          "element_type": "production_and_cost_explanation",
          "summary": "3 • Kamoa-Kakula’s improved cash costs (C1) were primarily due to the savings from the smelter operations. After producing the first batch of anodes in late Q4 2025, the smelter ramped up to 60% capacity during the quarter. All concentrates produced by the Phase 1, 2 and 3 concentrators during the quarter were either smelted at the Kamoa-Kakula smelter or at the Lualaba Copper Smelter (LCS) in Kolwezi. • Kamoa-Kakula’s Q1 2026 smelter operating costs averaged $0.27/lb., which were more than offset by $0.32/lb. in sulphuric acid by-product credits. In addition, logistics charges in Q1 2026 were significantly lower at $0.22/lb, compared with $0.70/lb. in Q4 2025. The logistics cost improvement was due to only exporting 99.7%-pure copper anodes in Q1 2026, compared to exporting 35% to 40% contained copper in concentrates in Q4 2025. • Kipushi sold a record 54,940 tonnes of zinc (net of payability) during the quarter , at an average realized zinc price of $1.47/lb., compared with 48,075 tonnes in Q4 2025 at an average realized zinc price of $1.44/lb. • Kipushi recognized a record quarterly revenue of $162 million, a segmented profit of $16 million and EBITDA of $58 million in Q1 2026, which is equivalent to a record EBITDA margin of 36% . This compares with an EBITDA of $44 million and an EBITDA margin of 32% in Q4 2025. • Kipushi’s cost of sales per pound of payable zinc sold was ",
